From 91d923c9f5813bb503544d80f9f7cc4cd8102e9a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Derek Ross <derekross@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:02:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 50/52] ipa: simple: agc: Choose the exposure on the predicted
 output

Daylight test 5 on the Google Pixel 7 Pro (libcamera 4.17): pictures
came out too dark (mean sRGB 35-92 against 80-109 from a reference
phone on the same scenes), and three main camera stills of eleven were
taken with the AE still searching after 1.5 s. The highlight limits
stepped against shares of clipped samples that jump with the exposure,
and the leniency for a dark rest of the picture flipped as the
exposure moved the rest across its thresholds, so the loop hunted.

Predict the picture instead. From the luminance histogram and the
peak histogram, compute at any exposure the mean output (through the
white balance and digital gains, the shoulder, the tone curve, the
contrast curve and the gamma), the share at sRGB 240 or more, and the
share the sensor saturates, and bisect for

- the exposure that brings the mean output to sRGB 102, and
- the highest one that keeps both shares within 2%, beyond what still
  clips two stops under the first: a lamp or the sun that no exposure
  saves no longer darkens the picture around it.

Take the lower, but at most two stops under the first, and step three
quarters of the way there in stops (three stops up from a very dark
picture, a sixteenth down from one pinned bright). As the predictions
move smoothly with the exposure, the loop settles.

In a model of the loop with the sensor a frame slower than modelled,
a sky scene, a lamp in a dark room and a lawn converge in 9-12 frames
without a reversal; the lamp scene reversed 33 times with the previous
limits.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
